WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.967
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:08.468
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.768 --> 00:00:15.809
Unit one: Academic Writing. Today you will write in a formal, compact academic style using nominalization.

4
00:00:16.109 --> 00:00:18.598
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:22.882
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:23.717 --> 00:00:24.602
Thesis.

7
00:00:25.617 --> 00:00:28.928
Thesis means the central claim that an essay sets out to defend.

8
00:00:29.517 --> 00:00:33.432
Her thesis challenges the assumption that technology always improves learning.

9
00:00:34.017 --> 00:00:36.332
Your turn. Say: Thesis.

10
00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:40.156
Thesis.

11
00:00:40.917 --> 00:00:41.860
Argument.

12
00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:46.127
Argument means a line of reasoning that leads to a conclusion.

13
00:00:46.717 --> 00:00:50.179
The argument rests on three carefully selected case studies.

14
00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:53.912
Your turn. Say: Argument.

15
00:00:56.767 --> 00:00:57.721
Argument.

16
00:00:58.483 --> 00:00:59.659
Citation.

17
00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:04.913
Citation means a formal reference to the source of an idea or quotation.

18
00:01:05.517 --> 00:01:09.142
Each statistic must be accompanied by an accurate citation.

19
00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:12.251
Your turn. Say: Citation.

20
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:16.124
Citation.

21
00:01:16.883 --> 00:01:18.186
Evidence-based.

22
00:01:19.183 --> 00:01:23.261
Evidence-based means grounded in systematic research rather than opinion.

23
00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:27.092
Ministries increasingly demand evidence-based policy.

24
00:01:27.683 --> 00:01:30.370
Your turn. Say: Evidence-based.

25
00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:34.687
Evidence-based.

26
00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:36.590
Coherence.

27
00:01:37.583 --> 00:01:41.336
Coherence means the logical connection of ideas across a text.

28
00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:44.704
Clear topic sentences give an essay coherence.

29
00:01:45.317 --> 00:01:47.656
Your turn. Say: Coherence.

30
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:51.698
Coherence.

31
00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:53.289
Critique.

32
00:01:54.283 --> 00:01:58.477
Critique means a detailed, balanced evaluation of a work or theory.

33
00:01:59.083 --> 00:02:02.360
His critique exposes a flaw in the original methodology.

34
00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:05.614
Your turn. Say: Critique.

35
00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:09.224
Critique.

36
00:02:09.983 --> 00:02:10.996
Scholarly.

37
00:02:11.983 --> 00:02:15.631
Scholarly means written by experts and based on rigorous study.

38
00:02:16.217 --> 00:02:19.412
Wikipedia is not regarded as a scholarly source.

39
00:02:20.017 --> 00:02:22.448
Your turn. Say: Scholarly.

40
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.440
Scholarly.

41
00:02:27.183 --> 00:02:28.463
Synthesis.

42
00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:33.516
Synthesis means the integration of several sources into a new whole.

43
00:02:34.117 --> 00:02:37.962
A literature review demands synthesis, not mere summary.

44
00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.469
Your turn. Say: Synthesis.

45
00:02:44.333 --> 00:02:45.671
Synthesis.

46
00:02:46.417 --> 00:02:47.360
Rhetoric.

47
00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:52.706
Rhetoric means the art of persuasive language, sometimes without substance.

48
00:02:53.317 --> 00:02:55.781
Behind the rhetoric, the proposal lacks detail.

49
00:02:56.383 --> 00:02:58.664
Your turn. Say: Rhetoric.

50
00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:02.362
Rhetoric.

51
00:03:03.117 --> 00:03:03.955
Coherent.

52
00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:08.447
Coherent means logical, consistent and easy to follow.

53
00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:11.479
She presented a coherent case for reform.

54
00:03:12.083 --> 00:03:14.503
Your turn. Say: Coherent.

55
00:03:17.367 --> 00:03:18.345
Coherent.

56
00:03:19.133 --> 00:03:22.505
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:27.837
Visal, your first draft was full of 'I think' and 'we found'. What did you change?

58
00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:36.873
I nominalised key verbs. Instead of 'we combined the evidence', I wrote: the synthesis of evidence strengthens the argument.

59
00:03:37.983 --> 00:03:40.563
Why is that more effective in academic writing?

60
00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:48.143
It shifts the focus from the writer to the ideas, and it packs more information into each clause.

61
00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:51.133
Is there a risk of overdoing it?

62
00:03:52.250 --> 00:04:01.079
Definitely. Too many abstract nouns make a text dense and unreadable, so I balance them with clear, active verbs.

63
00:04:02.233 --> 00:04:03.757
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:04.617 --> 00:04:06.930
Grammar focus: Nominalization.

65
00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:08.486
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:09.183 --> 00:04:11.961
The synthesis of evidence strengthens the argument.

67
00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:16.986
The rapid expansion of online learning has transformed higher education.

68
00:04:17.883 --> 00:04:20.939
The absence of reliable data undermines the conclusion.

69
00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:32.946
Nominalization turns processes into things: expand → expansion, absent → absence. It makes writing more formal and lets you build long, information-rich noun phrases.

70
00:04:33.683 --> 00:04:37.624
Be careful! Don't say: The analyse of the data was careful.

71
00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:42.367
Say: The analysis of the data was careful.

72
00:04:42.667 --> 00:04:46.548
Nominalise correctly: analyse → analysis.

73
00:04:47.333 --> 00:04:49.962
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:04:50.767 --> 00:04:52.244
Writing Like a Scholar.

75
00:04:52.544 --> 00:04:57.991
Academic prose differs from everyday writing less in its vocabulary than in its structure.

76
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:04.982
Writers tend to nominalise processes, so that 'we analysed the results' becomes 'the analysis of the results'.

77
00:05:05.967 --> 00:05:11.890
The synthesis of evidence strengthens the argument, because it shows how individual findings relate to one another.

78
00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:18.174
Coherence, however, depends on more than formal style; each paragraph must advance the thesis.

79
00:05:19.167 --> 00:05:24.893
Ultimately, rhetoric may impress readers, but only rigorous, well-cited critique will convince them.

80
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.118
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:30.983 --> 00:05:35.865
Choose the most academic version: 'Prices rose sharply, which worried consumers.'

82
00:05:36.165 --> 00:05:45.182
A, The sharp rise in prices caused consumer concern.. B, Prices went up a lot and people were worried.. C, Consumers were worried because prices rose..

83
00:05:48.733 --> 00:05:53.415
The answer is A: The sharp rise in prices caused consumer concern..

84
00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:57.571
The blank of reliable data undermines the conclusion.

85
00:05:57.871 --> 00:06:01.559
A, absent. B, absently. C, absence.

86
00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:06.960
The answer is C: absence.

87
00:06:08.117 --> 00:06:12.171
A literature review requires blank, not just a list of summaries.

88
00:06:12.471 --> 00:06:16.531
A, rhetoric. B, synthesis. C, citation.

89
00:06:20.067 --> 00:06:22.438
The answer is B: synthesis.

90
00:06:23.583 --> 00:06:25.386
Which collocation is correct?

91
00:06:25.686 --> 00:06:30.871
A, make a citation. B, provide a citation. C, do a citation.

92
00:06:34.433 --> 00:06:37.350
The answer is B: provide a citation.

93
00:06:38.517 --> 00:06:40.969
In the reading, what does coherence depend on?

94
00:06:41.269 --> 00:06:47.883
A, each paragraph advancing the thesis. B, using as many nouns as possible. C, impressive rhetoric.

95
00:06:51.433 --> 00:06:55.360
The answer is A: each paragraph advancing the thesis.

96
00:06:56.567 --> 00:06:59.753
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:00.617 --> 00:07:03.429
What makes academic writing persuasive and credible?

98
00:07:20.217 --> 00:07:21.705
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:22.005 --> 00:07:58.648
In my view, credibility comes first from evidence: every major claim should be supported by a reliable, properly cited source. Persuasiveness, on the other hand, depends on structure, with a clear thesis and paragraphs that build on one another. For instance, the synthesis of several studies is far more convincing than a single statistic. Some people believe elegant style is enough, but rhetoric without evidence quickly loses the reader's trust. So, ultimately, strong writing combines rigour with clarity.

100
00:07:58.948 --> 00:08:00.808
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:01.717 --> 00:08:06.296
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and nominalization.

102
00:08:06.596 --> 00:08:11.443
Today's value is dedication. Practise a little every day, and you will get better.

103
00:08:11.743 --> 00:08:14.105
See you in Unit two. Goodbye!

104
00:08:14.405 --> 00:08:18.183
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

